---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Privacy" description="MacPUMP keeps your data on your Mac. No account, no tracking and no network connections.">
  <div class="container container--narrow page">
    <header class="page__header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="page__title">Your data stays on your Mac.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        MacPUMP is built to watch your Mac without telling anyone else about it. Here is exactly what it does and doesn’t
        do.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The app</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No network connections.</strong> MacPUMP doesn’t connect to the internet. The only links it opens are ones you click, such as Help, and they open in your browser.</li>
        <li><strong>No account, analytics, telemetry or crash reporting.</strong> Nothing about you or your Mac is collected or sent anywhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Your normal user rights.</strong> MacPUMP installs no helper, daemon or kernel extension and never asks for an administrator password.</li>
        <li><strong>Read-only.</strong> It reads your Mac’s sensors and never changes a setting, including how your Mac charges.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What’s stored, and where</h2>
      <p>
        History is kept in <code>~/Library/Application Support/MacPUMP/</code>. The folder can only be opened by your user
        account, and so can the files in it. Power history is kept for 7 days by default, or 30 days if you choose.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can turn recording off, and see, export or delete your history at any time from <strong>Settings › Data &amp;
        Privacy</strong>. Exports are saved only where you choose, and you can hide app names in them. An optional debug
        log, if you turn it on, also stays on your Mac.
      </p>

      <h2>Support files</h2>
      <p>
        If you ever need help, MacPUMP can make a support file with identifying details removed. You can preview it before
        saving, and it goes nowhere unless you send it yourself.
      </p>

      <h2>Notifications</h2>
      <p>
        macOS asks once whether MacPUMP may show notifications, the first time you turn on a reminder or alert. If you never
        use them, you are never asked.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This website has no analytics, advertising or tracking cookies. It remembers your choice of light or dark appearance
        in your browser’s local storage, and that never leaves your device. Like any website, the hosting provider processes
        basic request information (such as your IP address) to deliver pages.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
